\name{pride_file_checksums}
\alias{pride_file_checksums}
\title{The MD5 and exact size PRIDE recorded for each file deposited in a project}
\usage{
pride_file_checksums(accession, timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{accession}{A project accession, e.g. \code{"PXD000001"}. Upper-cased and trimmed first, because
  PRIDE's checksum route is case-sensitive.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow for the whole call.
  \code{NULL}, the default, adds no limit of mzLibR's own, because mzLib already bounds every
  request: it retries a transient failure three times, after 5, 20 and 60 s, and each attempt
  can take up to 100 s. An outage that hangs is therefore reported as
  \code{mzlib_service_unavailable} after about 8 minutes per request, and an empty list costs a
  second request, the project lookup. A number cuts the whole call off after that many seconds
  with \code{mzlib_timeout}, which is not \code{mzlib_service_unavailable}.
}

}
\description{
List the MD5 and exact size PRIDE recorded for each file deposited in a project: the record a downloaded file is checked against.

Answers \strong{is the file on my disk the file PRIDE holds?} A file is PRIDE's when its length is
\code{size_bytes} and its MD5 is \code{md5}. \code{\link{pride_download}} and \code{\link{pride_download_files}} check neither
yet, so for files that matter, check them yourself: \code{file.size()} and \code{tools::md5sum()} are all
it takes, as the example shows.
}
\details{
\strong{This is a third list, not a view of either of the others.} It covers the files the submitter
deposited at the project's root. PRIDE's own \code{generated/} files have no row: a file without a row
cannot be checked, and that does not make it invalid. It names files \code{\link{pride_list_files}} omits
(5 of PXD000001's 12 rows on 2026-10-21). And it can name files that are not deposits at all:
PXD015239's lists an NFS temp file, \code{.nfs80360b91005fae5300002478}. Look up the files you have by
name; do not read the list as the project's contents.

The \code{checksum} column of \code{\link{pride_list_files}} is not this MD5. Where PRIDE fills it in, it is a
40-character SHA-1, and for every PXD000001 file it is empty.
}
\value{
A data.frame with one row per file, in file-name order, and the columns \code{file_name},
  \code{md5}, \code{size_bytes} and \code{project_accession}. \strong{Its row names are the file names}, so
  \code{checksums["README.txt", ]} looks a file up by the name it was downloaded under; they are
  case-sensitive, as PRIDE's names are.

  \code{file_name} is the bare file name, spelled as \code{\link{pride_list_files}} spells it, never a path.
  \code{md5} is 32 hexadecimal characters as PRIDE wrote them: lower case in every list seen, but not
  rewritten, so compare without regard to case (\code{tools::md5sum()} is lower case).
  \code{size_bytes} is the file's exact size in bytes, the length a download of it comes to, as a
  double (raw files pass 2 GiB). Compare a downloaded file's \code{file.size()} with it, not with
  \code{file_size_bytes} from \code{\link{pride_list_files}}, which can differ: for PXD000001's
  \code{PRIDE_Exp_Complete_Ac_22134.xml.gz} that one is 10,677,205 bytes and this one is 10,668,000,
  the length the download has. \code{project_accession} is the accession the list was fetched for.

  \strong{Zero rows when the project exists but PRIDE holds no checksums for it}: that is an answer,
  not an error, though nothing can then be checked. An unknown accession raises
  \code{mzlib_project_not_found}, and so does a project not yet published, since PRIDE holds no
  checksums before publication. A list mzLib cannot read (a new header, a malformed row, a name
  listed twice) stays an \code{mzlib_bridge_error} with \code{error_type} \code{"MzLibException"}: the project
  exists and its list is broken, so "check for a typo" would send you looking for the wrong
  mistake.

}
\section{Checking a download}{


Check both the length and the MD5. The length costs nothing; the MD5 reads the whole file. The
length alone does not prove the bytes: on 7 and 8 October 2026, EBI served about 23 of about 70
large transfers at the right size with its own error text written over part of them
(mzLib #1449). The PRIDE article shows the check on two damaged copies of a file.

}
